# Source: Lecture Quiz 2 (solutions), third question (the variable "fact" is renamed f).
id: factorial-wrong
title: A factorial loop with a bug
topic: Loops
level: 3
source: Lecture Quiz 2, factorial
tags: while, invariant, counterexample, product

task:
  The program below should compute the factorial of $n$. The postcondition
  uses $prod(k, 1, n, k) = 1 * 2 * ... * n$. The invariant
  $1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k)$ is proposed for the loop.

  Check the proposed invariant, and decide whether the triple is valid.

pre: i = 1 && f = 1 && n >= 1
post: f = prod(k, 1, n, k)

program:
  {P}
  {I}
  while (i < n) do {
    {I && i < n}
    {R2}
    f := f * i;      // b1
    {R1}
    i := i + 1       // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(i < n)}
  {Q}

verdict: invalid

step: The proposed invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  We take the invariant proposed in the task. The loop rule with
  consequence needs three checks, with the loop condition $B$ being
  $i < n$: establishment $P ==> I$, preservation of $I$ by the body, and
  exit $I && !B ==> Q$.
set: I = 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k)

step: Establishment of the proposed I
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
text:
  No statement comes before the loop, so we prove that the precondition
  $P$ implies $I$.
  >>> «i = 1 && f = 1 && n >= 1 : P» ==> «1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k) : I»
vc: i = 1 && f = 1 && n >= 1 ==> 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k)
why:
  With $i = 1$: $1 <= 1 <= n$ follows from $n >= 1$, and
  $prod(k, 1, 1, k) = 1 = f$.

step: Preservation of the proposed I: R1, the weakest precondition of i := i + 1
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  We check that one iteration keeps the invariant:
  >>> {«1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k) : I» && «i < n : B»} f := f * i; i := i + 1 {«1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k) : I»}
  We calculate the weakest precondition of the body for $I$, from the last
  statement. We substitute $i$ with $i + 1$ in $I$.
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, I)
  = (1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k))[i <- i + 1]
  = 1 <= i + 1 <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k)     // i is replaced by i + 1
  = 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k)     // subtract 1 from each part of the bounds
set: R1 = 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k)

step: Preservation of the proposed I: R2, the weakest precondition of f := f * i
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $f$ with $f * i$ in $R1$, computed in the previous step.
calc:
  wp(f := f * i, R1)
  = (0 <= i <= n - 1 && f = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k))[f <- f * i]
  = 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f * i = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k)     // f is replaced by f * i
set: R2 = 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f * i = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k)

step: Preservation of the proposed I fails
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
text:
  By the consequence rule, the triple holds when $I && B$ implies $R2$.
  >>> «1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k) : I» && «i < n : B» ==> «0 <= i <= n - 1 && f * i = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k) : R2»
vc: 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k) && i < n ==> 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f * i = prod(k, 1, i + 1, k)
status: fails
counterexample: i = 1, f = 1, n = 2
why:
  From $f = 1 * ... * i$ the body computes $f * i = 1 * ... * i * i$, but
  $R2$ needs $1 * ... * i * (i + 1)$. In the state $i = 1$, $f = 1$,
  $n = 2$: $f * i = 1$, and $prod(k, 1, 2, k) = 2$. So the proposed
  formula is not an invariant of the loop.

step: A corrected invariant I
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  We trace the loop from $i = 1$, $f = 1$:

  | Loop head | $i$ | $f$ |
  |---|---|---|
  | before iteration 1 | $1$ | $1$ |
  | before iteration 2 | $2$ | $1$ |
  | before iteration 3 | $3$ | $2$ |
  | before iteration 4 | $4$ | $6$ |

  The body multiplies $f$ by $i$ before it increases $i$, so $f$ is the
  product of the numbers below $i$: $f = 1 * 2 * ... * (i - 1)$. We keep
  the bounds of the proposed invariant.
set: I = 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k)

step: Establishment of the corrected I
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
text:
  We prove that the precondition $P$ implies the new $I$.
  >>> «i = 1 && f = 1 && n >= 1 : P» ==> «1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k) : I»
vc: i = 1 && f = 1 && n >= 1 ==> 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k)
why:
  With $i = 1$ the product $prod(k, 1, 0, k)$ has no term, so it is $1$,
  which is $f$. The bounds $1 <= 1 <= n$ follow from $n >= 1$.

step: Preservation of the corrected I: R1, the weakest precondition of i := i + 1
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b2
text:
  We check the triple:
  >>> {«1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k) : I» && «i < n : B»} f := f * i; i := i + 1 {«1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k) : I»}
  We substitute $i$ with $i + 1$ in the new $I$.
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, I)
  = (1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k))[i <- i + 1]
  = 1 <= i + 1 <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i + 1 - 1, k)     // i is replaced by i + 1
  = 1 <= i + 1 <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k)             // i + 1 - 1 is i
  = 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f = prod(k, 1, i, k)             // subtract 1 from each part of the bounds
set: R1 = 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f = prod(k, 1, i, k)

step: Preservation of the corrected I: R2, the weakest precondition of f := f * i
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: b1
text:
  We substitute $f$ with $f * i$ in $R1$, computed in the previous step.
calc:
  wp(f := f * i, R1)
  = (0 <= i <= n - 1 && f = prod(k, 1, i, k))[f <- f * i]
  = 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f * i = prod(k, 1, i, k)     // f is replaced by f * i
set: R2 = 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f * i = prod(k, 1, i, k)

step: Preservation of the corrected I holds
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
text:
  By the consequence rule, the triple holds when $I && B$ implies $R2$.
  >>> «1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k) : I» && «i < n : B» ==> «0 <= i <= n - 1 && f * i = prod(k, 1, i, k) : R2»
vc: 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k) && i < n ==> 0 <= i <= n - 1 && f * i = prod(k, 1, i, k)
why:
  From $1 <= i$ we get $0 <= i$, and for integers $i < n$ is $i <= n - 1$.
  Since $i >= 1$, $prod(k, 1, i, k) = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k) * i$, which is
  $f * i$ by $I$.

step: Exit with the corrected I fails
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
text:
  When the loop stops, $I$ holds and $B$ is false. This must imply the
  postcondition $Q$.
  >>> «1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k) : I» && «!(i < n) : ¬B» ==> «f = prod(k, 1, n, k) : Q»
vc: 1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i - 1, k) && !(i < n) ==> f = prod(k, 1, n, k)
status: fails
counterexample: i = 2, f = 1, n = 2
why:
  $!(i < n)$ and $i <= n$ give $i = n$, so $I$ gives
  $f = 1 * ... * (n - 1)$, which is not $1 * ... * n$ when $n >= 2$.
text:
  A failed check shows only that this invariant does not prove the triple.
  Here the state $i = 2$, $f = 1$, $n = 2$ is reached by the program: from
  $i = 1$, $f = 1$, $n = 2$ the loop runs once and stops. So the triple
  itself is not valid.
vc: {i = 1 && f = 1 && n >= 1} while (i < n) do { f := f * i; i := i + 1 } {f = prod(k, 1, n, k)}
status: fails
counterexample: i = 1, f = 1, n = 2
why:
  From this state the program ends with $i = 2$ and $f = 1$, but
  $prod(k, 1, 2, k) = 2$.
result: The triple is not valid: the program computes $(n - 1)!$ instead of $n!$.

summary:
  The failed preservation check of the proposed invariant was a sign of a
  bug in the program. Swapping the two statements of the body,
  `i := i + 1; f := f * i`, gives a correct program, for which
  $1 <= i <= n && f = prod(k, 1, i, k)$ is an invariant.
